%N A076227 Number of surviving Collatz residues mod 2^n.
%C A076227 Number of residue classes in which A074473(m) is not constant.
%C A076227 The ratio of numbers of inhomogenous r-classes versus uniform-classes enumerated here increases with n and tends to 0. For n large enough ratio < a(16)/65536 = 2114/65536 ~ 3.23%.
%C A076227 Theorem: a(n) can be generated for each n > 2 algorithmically in a Pascal's triangle-like manner from the two starting values 0 and 1. This result is based on the fact that the Collatz residues (mod 2^k) can be evolved according to a binary tree. There is a direct connectedness to A100982, A056576, A022921, A020915. - _Mike Winkler_, Sep 12 2017
%C A076227 Brown's criterion ensures that the sequence is complete (see formulae). - _Vladimir M. Zarubin_, Aug 11 2019
%C A076227 a(n) is the number of binary words v(1)v(2)...v(n) such that 2^m < 3^(v(1)+...+v(m)) for all m = 1..n; a(0) = 1 counts the empty word. - _Kazunobu Hikawa_, Jul 21 2026
%C A076227 From _Philippe Cochin_, Sep 21 2026: (Start)
%C A076227 This counting problem is Terras's. Definition 1.12 of his 1976 paper calls a binary word admissible when 3^(v(1)+...+v(m)) > 2^m at every initial truncation, and active when that also holds at the full length; Definition 1.13 counts the admissible words of length k having j zeros, calling this a modified binomial coefficient. Then a(n) counts the active words of length n, and the table in the EXAMPLE section refines that count, its row n being the length and its column k one more than the number of ones.
%C A076227 The recursion underlying the Pascal-type theorem in the comment of _Mike Winkler_ above appears already in Terras 1976, as his Theorem 1.14 (Modified binomial recursion). Write n(j,k) for the number of admissible words of length k having j zeros, and put c(j,k) = 1 if j < k*(1 - log(2)/log(3)) and c(j,k) = 0 otherwise. Then with n(0,1) = 1 and n(1,1) = 0, c(j,k)*n(j,k) + c(j-1,k)*n(j-1,k) = n(j,k+1). The gate c(j,k) says that a length-k word with j zeros is still active, so this is Pascal's rule restricted to the surviving words; without the gate one counts all 2^k words.
%C A076227 Terras's Corollary 1.16 is Sum_{j=0..k} n(j,k)/2^k = F(k), with n(j,k) as above, where F is his distribution function F(k) = lim_{m->oo} (1/m)*|{x <= m : chi(x) >= k}| for the stopping time chi, and his Theorem 1.17 proves that F(k) converges monotonically to 0. In this sequence's terms F(k) = a(k-1)/2^(k-1) for k >= 2, not a(k)/2^k: the admissible words of length k split into the a(k) active ones and the terminal ones that first fail at k, and the two classes together number 2*a(k-1). His Table A (p. 247) tabulates 2*F(k) for k = 10, 20, ..., 90 and k = 100, 200, ..., 900. (End)
